module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.IdealSheaf.Basic
public import Architect
public import Hironaka.Ch2.GradedIdeal
public import Hironaka.Compat.InvertibleNonzeroGenerator

/-!
# Ch. II §2, Corollary 2 of Theorem 2 (p. 197), in the printed form

> Corollary 2. Let V be an algebraic scheme which is imbedded in a non-singular algebraic scheme X.
> Let us suppose that the sheaf of ideals of V on X is invertible, i.e., locally everywhere
> generated by a single non-zero element. Let W be a non-singular irreducible subscheme of V. Then V
> is normally flat along W if and only if V is equi-multiple along W, i.e., V has a constant
> multiplicity at the points of W.  (p. 197)

`Subscheme.isNormallyFlatAlong'_iff_isEquimultipleAlong` (`Hironaka/Ch2/GradedIdeal.lean`) proves it for
`Subscheme.IsInvertibleIdeal` (local generators that are non-zero-divisors).  This module states the
corollary with the printed hypothesis "locally everywhere generated by a single non-zero element"
(`Subscheme.isNormallyFlatAlong'_iff_isEquimultipleAlong_of_forall_exists_ne_zero`), derived from it by
`Subscheme.isInvertibleIdeal_iff_forall_exists_ne_zero` (`Hironaka/Compat/InvertibleNonzeroGenerator.lean`).
The blueprint node `cor:II.2-thm2-2` is on the printed form.
-/

@[expose] public section

open AlgebraicGeometry

namespace Hironaka

universe u

/-- Corollary 2 of Theorem 2, p. 197, as printed: "Let `V` be an algebraic scheme which is imbedded in a
non-singular algebraic scheme `X`.  Let us suppose that the sheaf of ideals of `V` on `X` is invertible,
i.e., locally everywhere generated by a single non-zero element.  Let `W` be a non-singular irreducible
subscheme of `V`.  Then `V` is normally flat along `W` if and only if `V` is equi-multiple along `W`,
i.e., `V` has a constant multiplicity at the points of `W`."

Encoding: as for `Subscheme.isNormallyFlatAlong'_iff_isEquimultipleAlong`.  "Locally everywhere generated
by a single non-zero element" is: every point `x` has an affine neighbourhood `U` and a section `a` of
`Γ(X, U)` with `V(U) = (a)` and non-zero germ at `x`.  (A mere `a ≠ 0` in `Γ(X, U)` would not do: on
`X = X₁ ⊔ X₂` the ideal `(0, 1)` of the component `X₁` is generated by a non-zero section, but the ideal
sheaf of `X₁` is zero near its points.)

The proof is the paper's through `Subscheme.isNormallyFlatAlong'_iff_isEquimultipleAlong`: on a
non-singular scheme the printed hypothesis is equivalent to the invertibility used there
(`Subscheme.isInvertibleIdeal_iff_forall_exists_ne_zero`).

Deviation from the paper (gap filled): the paper uses "invertible, i.e., locally everywhere generated by
a single non-zero element" as one notion; their equivalence on a non-singular `X` is proved. -/
@[blueprint "cor:II.2-thm2-2"
  (statement := /-- Let $V$ be an algebraic scheme which is imbedded in a non-singular
    algebraic scheme $X$. Let us suppose that the sheaf of ideals of $V$ on $X$ is invertible,
    i.e., locally everywhere generated by a single non-zero element. Let $W$ be a non-singular
    irreducible subscheme of $V$. Then $V$ is normally flat along $W$
    (\cref{def:0.1-normally-flat}) if and only if $V$ is equi-multiple along $W$
    (\cref{def:equimultiple-along}), i.e., $V$ has a constant multiplicity at the points of
    $W$. (Corollary 2, p.~197.) Formalization note (encoding): ``locally everywhere generated by a
    single non-zero element'' is: every point has an affine neighbourhood $U$ and a section $a$ of
    $\Gamma(X,U)$ with $V(U)=(a)$ and non-zero germ at the point. -/)
  (proof := /-- (The paper's argument, p.~197: \cref{cor:II.2-thm2-2-invertible}.) Let $x$ be an
    arbitrary point of $W$ and $O$ the local ring of $V$ at $x$. Then, by the assumption, we have a
    regular local ring $R$ and an element $f$ of $R$ such that $O=R/(f)R$. Let $P$ be the prime ideal
    in $R$ such that $\bar P=P/(f)R$ is the ideal of $W$ in $O$. Let $M$ be the maximal ideal of $R$.
    By Corollary~1 of Theorem~2, $\operatorname{gr}_{\bar P}(O)$ is flat over $O/\bar P$ (i.e., $V$ is
    normally flat along $W$ at $x$) if and only if $\nu_M(f)=\nu_P(f)$. This proves the assertion of
    Corollary~2 because $\nu_M(f)$ (resp.\ $\nu_P(f)$) is equal to the multiplicity of $V$ at $x$ (resp.\
    at the generic point of $W$). Here it should be remarked that $\nu_P(f)=\nu_{PR_P}(f)$ because of
    the regularity of $R/P$.

    \textbf{In Lean.} On a non-singular scheme, ``locally everywhere generated by a single non-zero
    element'' is equivalent to the invertibility used in \cref{cor:II.2-thm2-2-invertible}
    (\cref{lem:II.2-invertible-nonzero-generator}), and the corollary follows from that one. -/)
  (proofUses := ["cor:II.2-thm2-2-invertible", "lem:II.2-invertible-nonzero-generator"])
  (latexEnv := "corollary")]
theorem Subscheme.isNormallyFlatAlong'_iff_isEquimultipleAlong_of_forall_exists_ne_zero
    {X : Scheme.{u}} [IsAlgebraicScheme X] (hX : X.IsNonsingular) (V W : Subscheme X)
    (hV : ∀ x : X, ∃ (U : X.affineOpens) (hx : x ∈ (U : X.Opens)) (a : Γ(X, U)),
      V.ideal U = Ideal.span {a} ∧ X.presheaf.germ U x hx a ≠ 0)
    (hW : W.IsNonsingular) (hirr : W.IsIrreducible) (hWV : W.IsContainedIn V) :
    V.IsNormallyFlatAlong' W ↔ (Subscheme.totalTransform V.ι W).IsEquimultipleAlong := by
  have := IsAlgebraicScheme.isNoetherian X
  exact Subscheme.isNormallyFlatAlong'_iff_isEquimultipleAlong hX V W
    ((Subscheme.isInvertibleIdeal_iff_forall_exists_ne_zero hX V).mpr hV) hW hirr hWV

end Hironaka
